WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.360
A Diophantine equation asks for integer solutions, not merely points on a continuous curve

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:15.300
Six x plus nine y equals fifteen draws a line, but only the points with integer coordinates meet the arithmetic requirement.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.797
Both coefficients and the target share a factor of three, so divide the entire equation by three

00:00:23.797 --> 00:00:27.183
The simpler equation has the solution one, one

00:00:27.183 --> 00:00:33.533
This legal division preserves every solution because it divides an equality by a nonzero constant.

00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:43.034
Increasing x by three adds six to twice x, while decreasing y by two subtracts six from three y

00:00:43.034 --> 00:00:44.329
The changes cancel

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:51.667
Every integer t therefore gives a solution, and subtracting two solutions shows this parameterization captures them all.

00:00:52.967 --> 00:01:01.564
Changing the target to fourteen destroys solvability: the left side is always divisible by three, while fourteen is not

00:01:01.564 --> 00:01:11.067
More generally, an integer linear equation has integer solutions exactly when the greatest common divisor of its coefficients divides its target.
